{"doc_id": "doc43169", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Miller Lite is considered the first mainstream light beer in the United States. Its origins trace back to 1967 when biochemist Joseph L. Owades, Ph.D., developed “Gablinger’s Diet Beer” for New York’s Rheingold Brewery. Owades later passed the recipe to Peter Hand Brewing in Chicago, which was soon renamed Meister Brau Brewing and began selling the product as Meister Brau Lite alongside its flagship brew. After Meister Brau ran into financial trouble, the company sold several of its labels to Miller in 1972. Miller re‑introduced the beer simply as “Lite” in test markets in 1973 and, in 1975, launched it nationally under the name Miller Lite. The brand’s heavy advertising campaign helped it quickly become a leading light‑beer offering in the American market.", "reason": "missing_gold_answer", "seed": 791695745, "similarity": null, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "Miller Lite was essentially the first mainstream light beer in the United States. The brew originated as “Gablinger’s Diet Beer,” created in 1967 by biochemist Joseph L. Owades for New York’s Rheingold Brewery. Owades later gave the recipe to Chicago’s Peter Hand Brewing, which introduced it as Meister Brau Lite. After Meister Brau Brewing sold the brand to Miller in 1972, the beer was relaunched simply as “Lite” and, in 1973, test‑marketed in Springfield, Illinois; Knoxville, Tennessee; and San Diego, California. The national rollout occurred in 1975 under the name Miller Lite, establishing it as the pioneering light beer on the American market.", "reason": "missing_gold_answer", "seed": 1201071664, "similarity": null, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "37aee9a15b6b07f9ff982dc1dcaae38432ba847e387c59e434bd58970e207c28", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "Miller Lite", "matched_aliases": ["Miller Lite"], "query_id": "test1210", "question_hash": "1795eb830419